<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord of the Isles</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dominus Insularum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lord of the Isles, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Dominus Insularum, was the title of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clan-donald">Clan Donald</a> rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and western Highlands, first used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-islay">John of Islay</a> in 1336. The lords ruled almost as independent princes until James IV forfeited the lordship in 1493. The title was later annexed to the heir of the Scottish crown and is now held by the heir apparent to the British throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-of-the-isles.jpg" alt="Lord of the Isles" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_Lord_of_the_Isles.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
